From: Majid Arasskhani <M.Arasskhani@gosilkroad.net>
Date: Tue, 15 Sep 2026 05:54:33 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 13/19] fix(frontend): two regressions in the chat sync rewrite
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Review of 27a53d4 found the clientMessageId diff could duplicate messages,
and the merge could drop an edit that had not reached the server yet.

Duplicates: a local row whose id is missing was treated as new. That is
exactly what an image or speech placeholder becomes when the flow replaces
it in place with an error notice — the replacement is built without an id,
while the server already holds the row it replaced, so the next sync
appended a second copy instead of patching the first. Rows without an id now
pair with the next unmatched server row of the same role, which also covers
the pre-clientMessageId rows the old positional diff handled.

The diff is also restricted to a contiguous tail. The append API adds at the
end, so re-sending a row from the middle would move it there and scramble
the order; skipping it is the safer failure. That also makes a partial
server window safe to diff against, so the walk now stops as soon as it has
enough rows to cover the local list — an ordinary turn is one request again,
not one per 200 messages of history. The full-replace PUT still reads the
whole history and refuses to run truncated.

Merge: taking every field from the newer copy meant a model switch or tool
toggle could be reverted by a refresh that arrived before the pending PATCH
went out — the server row is newer precisely because it has not seen the
change yet. A session with queued appends or metadata keeps its local
fields, as it did before the rewrite.

